  "question": {
    "text": "A certain orbital has <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></math>&nbsp;and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mo>-</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>.</mo></math> The number of radial nodes in this orbital is _______. (Round off to the Nearest Integer).",
    "image": null
  },
  "options": [],
  "correctValue": "0",
  "solution": {
    "text": "<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></math>&nbsp;and&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#8467;</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mo>-</mo><mn>3</mn></math></p><p>Hence, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#8467;</mi></math> value must be <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>3</mn><mo>.</mo></math></p><p>Now, number of radial nodes&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mo>-</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#8467;</mi><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>-</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></math></p>",
